00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:51.970
So first off, The Night Before Our Stars was
a special preview screening and Q&amp;A session

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:58.589
with Shailene Woodley, Ansel Elgort, John
Green and bunch of other people from the movie.

00:00:58.589 --> 00:00:59.979
They did it in theaters all over the world
and just broadcast the Q&amp;A to all the places

00:00:59.979 --> 00:01:01.670
at the same time.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.900
If you're a fellow nerdfighter then you'll
be pleased to know that they showed John Green's

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:11.270
cameo in the film. It got cut for time and
even though he said he was happy they cut

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:16.120
it out of the movie. All of nerdfighteria
will really enjoy it when the dvd comes out.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:21.410
The place I went to was understandably mostly
teenage women and families but it was a very

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.500
different experience from the time I saw that
last Twilight movie in a theater. That time

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:30.800
it was pretty much all women too, but instead
of everyone crying everyone was laughing.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:37.800
At Twilight, not at TFIOS. Although the Fault
In Our Stars had some really funny moments.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.480
It's a really good movie to go see if you're
1 not afraid to cry and 2 want a really deep

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.750
emotional experience that you might not get
at a big effects driven action movie. And

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:52.110
3, it's always fun to watch a movie about
a book you really love. Just to watch it unfold

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.380
in front of your eyes.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.900
I caution you, if you are a huge fan of the
book you're eyes will either burn out of their

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.010
sockets from all the excitement or melt from
all the tears.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:04.049
One of my favorite things about the movie
was that it's totally upfront about what it

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:09.160
is. A young adult cancer drama intended to
yank on your heartstrings and ask the question,

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.780
what happens to the people you leave behind.
But it gets there in a very authentic way.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:19.810
I kept having to squish old feelings the movie
reminded me of when I was around 16 to 18.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:24.170
I guess if you wanted to explain the story
to someone in an even simpler way you could

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.430
just say "pain demands to be felt"

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.310
I'm pretty good at keeping feels deep down
inside, but if you ever wanted to be a part

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:35.660
of a giant group of people all feeling the
same thing. This will be the best movie you

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:36.920
go see all summer.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.810
For all the book readers out there I'll talk
more about specific stuff the movie changed

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.480
during spoilers in case you want to wait till
you see it.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:50.290
But even if you don't consider yourself a
nerdfighter DFTA and go see this movie. This

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.940
will go down as the highest grossing movie
originated by a youtuber. And the community

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:59.840
of nerdfighters is a big reason why everyone
knows about this film. I won't make any comparisons,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.170
because it wasn't like any other movie I've
seen in the last year. And that's a very good

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:03.950
thing.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.680
So now I'm going to movie into spoilers and
talk about my Top 5 Moments and all the changes

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.090
from the book. So careful if you haven't seen
it yet. Here we go.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:16.290
Number 5 - Augustus Passionately Defends Price
of Dawn

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:20.530
This reminded me of every time I tried to
convince my girlfriends over the years to

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:25.130
watch Star Wars or Lord of The Rings. I was
also a huge fan of the Dragonlance books in

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:29.569
Middle School which is kind of like a Dungeons
and Dragons series of novels. I was never

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:34.140
quite as articulate as Augustus. He really
knows how to sell a book, regardless of what

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:34.560
it's about.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.220
Number 4 - Willem Dafoe as Van Houten

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:40.970
He was awful and funny at the same time. Which
was totally on purpose, the character is so

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:46.400
terrible all you can do is laugh at him. Especially
when he jumped in Hazel's car at the funeral.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.700
Be sure to let me know in the comments if
you ever listened to Swedish Hip Hop after

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.520
reading the book or seeing the movie.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.280
Remember, it's not important what they're
saying, it's what they're feeling.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.580
Number 3 - Hazel and Augustus's Talk While
Isaac Smashes Stuff

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.160
I laughed a lot while all this was going on,
mostly because Augustus has this unbroken

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.620
attention on Hazel and their conversation
about An Imperial Affliction and you see Hazel's

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:13.670
eyes dart back and forth between trophy smashing
and Augustus. Like she's surprised both by

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:18.669
Isaac going crazy and the fact that Augustus
is totally able to ignore it.

00:04:18.669 --> 00:04:22.309
Just on a technical level I can't imagine
how hard it must have been for the actors

00:04:22.309 --> 00:04:26.180
to do that scene and remember all their lines.
It would be like having two conversations

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:31.659
at the same time and remembering everything
without messing up.

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:34.120
Number 2 - The Birth of the Okays

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:37.849
That whole initial text montage and the way
the movie visually showed their texts was

00:04:37.849 --> 00:04:42.270
really cool. But I feel like the okays were
one of the most important parts of the story.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.710
Like that was like the real moment they became
bonded as a couple. I thought that was way

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:51.020
more meaningful and emotional than when they
actually had sex.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:55.009
But let me know what moment you think they
became an actual couple.

00:04:55.009 --> 00:04:58.469
and my Number 1 Moment - The Dinner in Amsterdam

00:04:58.469 --> 00:05:01.960
I felt like this was the real climax of the
movie. Because up to this point Hazel finally

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:06.379
gotten over her grenade fears and just let
herself be in love with Augustus who is just

00:05:06.379 --> 00:05:10.729
on fire. Ansel Elgort really did bring it.

00:05:10.729 --> 00:05:13.039
The really intimate way they filmed the restaurant
scene just made that whole dinner feel like

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:17.110
they were floating in heaven. And I liked
that they used the lights on the tree as a

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:21.539
visual metaphor for the title. I don't think
Dom Perignon tastes like stars but in that

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:24.469
moment in space and time it totally felt like
it did.

00:05:24.469 --> 00:05:29.309
But now it's your turn, let me know in the
comments what your favorite moment was?

